WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.500
Can I share that we both missed our turns on the way to work today and then we were both late?

9
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:34.100
Yeah.

10
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.200
It was so crazy.

11
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.720
I was talking to my mom and I missed my exit.

12
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:42.180
We were just, like, jabbering away and then I was like, oh, no, Mom, I gotta go.

13
00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.080
I've gone the wrong way.

14
00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:50.420
Well, I saw a text from you pop up on my little screen and I played it, you know, wireless and everything.

15
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.180
Just saying.

16
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:51.960
Yes, yes, folks.

17
00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.240
It was a message where it was like, you know, in the robot voice.

18
00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:56.560
Yeah, because I use Siri.

19
00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.280
Yes, telling me that you missed your turn off the freeway.

20
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.020
I called it my stupid exit.

21
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.580
I missed the stupid exit.

22
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:12.160
Well, while I was listening to that, I guess I missed my turn because all of a sudden I was at, like, this giant, I don't know.

23
00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:13.140
Like a shopping mall?

24
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.740
Yes, it had a Staples and a McDonald's and I thought, I've never seen this before.

25
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:17.440
Where am I?

26
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.460
I had gone almost a mile out of my way.

27
00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:20.320
I had to turn around.

28
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.300
So, I don't know what that says about today's podcast, but we both missed our turns.

29
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.200
Let's get to the episode today.

30
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.740
It is Dwight K.

31
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:30.800
Schrute, acting manager.

32
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:32.160
Acting in parentheses.

33
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:39.160
It is season seven, episode 24, written by Justin Spitzer and directed by Troy Miller.

34
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.160
I get very excited for episodes written by Justin.

35
00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.120
I think he is so funny.

36
00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:45.920
He's so funny.

37
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.100
He has now gone on to create two amazing television shows, Superstore and American Auto.

38
00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:51.540
Yeah.

39
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:52.900
Here's your summary.

40
00:01:53.620 --> 00:02:00.200
Acting manager Dwight is drunk on power and his coworkers are not happy with the changes he has implemented in the office.

41
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:05.080
But an incident with a firearm might save them from his reign of terror.

42
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.040
Reign of terror is some strong language.

43
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.780
Meanwhile, Gabe is struggling with his breakup with Aaron.

44
00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.140
Gabe kind of broke my heart.

45
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:16.920
Very much.

46
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.300
Fast fact number one.

47
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.560
This was our first episode without Steve or Will.

48
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:29.440
And according to Wikipedia, the fewest people tuned into this with the exception of Todd Packer.

49
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.980
Todd Packer was our lowest watched episode.

50
00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.140
Am I saying that right?

51
00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:35.680
This was our second loweSt.

52
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.480
Our lowest viewed episode was Todd Packer.

53
00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.740
Well, Miles McNutt would agree with that.

54
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:43.560
But I disagree with this episode.

55
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.580
I think this is a fantastic episode.

56
00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:49.840
Listen, people didn't watch it, but critics loved it.

57
00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:50.040
Oh.

58
00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.800
Including Miles McNutt.

59
00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:52.600
Yay.

60
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:53.680
He gave it a B.

61
00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.480
He called it, quote, a nice step forward for the show.

62
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.640
Alan Sepinwall from Hitflix said, quote,

63
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.960
I said I didn't really want to start to judge the post-Corelle version of the show until D'Angelo was gone.

64
00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:09.420
Well, he's gone.

65
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.920
And the first real impression of the show after Michael is a strong one.

66
00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:14.460
Yay.

67
00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:19.000
Many people called it the best episode of the series in years.

68
00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:20.760
I agree.

69
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.820
I loved this episode.

70
00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:23.560
Same.

71
00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:32.260
And I listened to the DVD commentary for this episode, which features Ellie Kemper, Mindy Kaling, Steve Healy, Justin Spitzer, and myself.

72
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.060
And we were all gushing about how everyone loved it.

73
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.900
The cast, the crew, writers, we were all thrilled with this episode.

74
00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:46.680
This was a big bump in our confidence, this episode, because we felt like, oh, wait, maybe we can do this.

75
00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:47.200
Yeah.

76
00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:56.740
Fast fact number two, officetelly.com has a great interview with Rain and Justin about this episode.

77
00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:57.960
It is really good.

78
00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:04.320
And in that interview, Justin was asked how he came up with the title of this episode, because it is very long.

79
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:15.660
He explained that the working title for this script was Interim Manager, but that Mindy said that that was such a boring title that some Tevos might refuse to record it.

80
00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.360
But she's not wrong.

81
00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.220
So when Justin sat down to write the script, he named it Dwight K.

82
00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:22.700
Schrute.

83
00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.100
Then he changed it to Dwight K.

84
00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.500
Schrute, colon, Interim Manager.

85
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:33.600
But then he did some research, and he realized that Dwight's actual title would be more of an acting manager.

86
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.900
And he figured that Dwight would want to downplay the acting manager part.

87
00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:41.540
So that's why he put acting in parentheses.

88
00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.840
Look how much thought went into the title of this episode.

89
00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:45.280
Just the title.

90
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:46.840
Thank you so much.

91
00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:50.660
Now, fast fact number three, everyone, buckle in.

92
00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:51.600
It's a deep dive.

93
00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:52.780
Oh, I can't wait.

94
00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:53.900
Wait, I want to guess.

95
00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.760
I want to guess, having watched the episode.

96
00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:01.200
It's going to be a deep dive on, well, I know the first one that comes to my mind.

97
00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:02.000
What's that?

98
00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:10.540
Either the samurai suit or the piranha or, wait, there's one other one.

99
00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:11.440
You said it.

100
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:12.200
I said it.

101
00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:13.040
The samurai suit.

102
00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:13.840
Piranha.

103
00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:14.780
The piranha.

104
00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:16.920
In this episode, Dwight.

105
00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:17.940
Oh, I can't wait.

106
00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:21.900
In this episode, Dwight has a piranha in his office.

107
00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:22.400
Mm-hmm.

108
00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:25.920
I rewatched this episode with my daughter and my mother-in-law.

109
00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:26.480
Okay.

110
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.840
And my mother-in-law, Lynn, travels a lot.

111
00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.620
And she said, that is not a piranha.

112
00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:33.660
Oh, really?

113
00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:34.000
Yeah.

114
00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:35.120
And I paused the TV.

115
00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:36.400
And I said, what do you mean?

116
00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.020
She goes, I am telling you, it's not a piranha.

117
00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:39.960
I've seen a piranha and that is not a piranha.

118
00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:40.880
They're smaller.

119
00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.420
And I said, oh, well, I don't know.

120
00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.760
Well, Angela, Lynn is right.

121
00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:47.480
Really?

122
00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.900
It is not a piranha in that tank.

123
00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:56.400
We could not have a real piranha because they are illegal to own in the state of California.

124
00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:59.060
Lynn, you knew it right away.

125
00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:05.160
Now, they are legal in Pennsylvania, so it is okay for Dwight to have a piranha in his office.

126
00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.920
But Randy told us that our trainers were not licensed to have piranhas.

127
00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:16.020
You can only have them in like a research facility or like a controlled environment.

128
00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.420
Like there is, I guess, a piranha in the Monterey Bay Aquarium,

129
00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:24.640
but they are on the restricted animals list for California.

130
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.580
By the way, I looked up what the other restricted animals are in California.

131
00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:29.640
I just got curious.

132
00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.200
Like what else can't you have?

133
00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:31.880
Can I guess?

134
00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:32.200
Sure.

135
00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:32.840
Let's see.

136
00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:33.780
Tiger.

137
00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:37.080
Well, no one can have a tiger.

138
00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:38.380
Oh, okay.

139
00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:38.920
I mean.

140
00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.020
Well, I watched that Netflix show.

141
00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:42.960
They have a ton of them in Florida and Oklahoma.

142
00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.080
Oh, I guess that's true.

143
00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:45.740
Okay.

144
00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:47.100
Well, that wasn't on the liSt.

145
00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:48.980
Some type of snake?

146
00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.180
Also not on the liSt.

147
00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:52.040
No snakes?

148
00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.840
No, you can have a snake and a tiger.

149
00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:56.040
I guess.

150
00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:56.640
I don't know.

151
00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:57.760
Here's what's on the liSt.

152
00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:58.880
I'll stop guessing.

153
00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:08.000
As pets, you are not allowed to have a ferret, a mongoose, a bat, a bear, gerbils.

154
00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:09.200
Gerbils?

155
00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:09.680
Mm-hmm.

156
00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.600
I had gerbils growing up and I loved them.

157
00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:13.840
They were so sweet.

158
00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:14.300
Yeah.

159
00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:19.120
And I wanted to get my kids gerbils because I had such fond memories of having my gerbil.

160
00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:19.580
Sure.

161
00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:20.760
Not in California.

162
00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:21.340
No, ma'am.

163
00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:22.320
What about a hamster?

164
00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:22.370
Sure.

165
00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.100
Certain hamsters are allowed and certain are not.

166
00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:26.920
Okay.

167
00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.840
Also, you cannot own a monkey or a hedgehog.

168
00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:32.200
Who wants to own a hedgehog?

169
00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:33.540
They're very cute.

170
00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.340
But you should not own a hedgehog.

171
00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:35.560
Yeah.

172
00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:37.400
Let them live in the wild.

173
00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:38.300
Let them live their life.

174
00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.180
There's enough animals that already want to live with us.

175
00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:41.480
Yeah.

176
00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.520
Well, anyway, so also on that list, you can't have a piranha.

177
00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:51.880
So what they provided us with was a paku, which is a very similar species.

178
00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:53.440
It's also found in South America.

179
00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:56.380
Paku look very similar to piranha.

180
00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:00.240
Randy said it would take an expert to notice the difference.

181
00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:01.860
Lynn Snyder.

182
00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:02.500
Yes.

183
00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.860
Randy also shared with us that our head trainer, Denise Sanders, brought an assortment of tanks

184
00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.500
and water filtration warming systems and set them up backstage.

185
00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:13.840
I remember that.

186
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:14.420
I do, too.

187
00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:16.300
I remember the tanks and past fish.

188
00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:16.660
Yes.

189
00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.300
We had four identical-looking paku.

190
00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.040
We used one on each day.

191
00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:29.460
And the total cost for all the tanks, equipment, the paku, and the trainers was $3,375.

192
00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:32.120
How do you train a paku?

193
00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:33.660
I don't know.

194
00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:35.340
I don't know.

195
00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.060
I have questions.

196
00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:39.420
I did not get that far into it.

197
00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:41.180
But I did not stop here.

198
00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.280
I did get curious about the piranha as a fish.

199
00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:44.780
Mm-hmm.

200
00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:46.920
Here's what I found out.

201
00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:52.340
I found out that while piranhas are dangerous and they can bite you if you're not careful,

202
00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:57.380
that idea that they just, like, swim around in packs and, like, eat a person to the bones.

203
00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:57.700
Yeah.

204
00:08:57.900 --> 00:08:59.160
That's kind of a myth.

205
00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:00.460
Sounds like a cartoon.

206
00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:01.480
Yeah.

207
00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.840
Piranha are actually really timid.

208
00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:04.760
They're kind of nervous.

209
00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:09.500
And the reason that they swim all together, it's not because they're, like, pack hunters.

210
00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:12.360
It's because they feel safe.

211
00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:15.600
That's why most little fish swim in, like, big groups.

212
00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:16.000
Right.

213
00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:16.340
Yeah.

214
00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:17.020
Exactly.

215
00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:21.760
However, splashing attracts piranha.

216
00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.040
I did not know this.

217
00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:28.260
This is why they think that maybe children get bitten more often because they're splashing.

218
00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:39.120
Also, if you're swimming in an area that might have piranha, don't swim near fishermen because the flapping of the fish that they're catching attracts piranha.

219
00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.860
Just a hot tip for you.

220
00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:41.260
Okay.

221
00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.020
If you're in a piranha stream.

222
00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:43.460
Okay.

223
00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:45.440
Or possible piranha stream.

224
00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:48.080
That said, people have been killed by piranha.

225
00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:49.100
It happens.

226
00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.820
It is not common.

227
00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.160
But I did get curious about what animal kills the most people each year.

228
00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.600
This took a turn I did not see coming.

229
00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.400
Listen, I got so curious.

230
00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.940
I was like, what animals can I not own?

231
00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:05.720
What animals are most dangerous?

232
00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:06.200
All right.

233
00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:06.460
Wait.

234
00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:07.700
I want to guess again.

235
00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.740
And I want to think outside the box here.

236
00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:12.740
I don't want to go with what might be some obvious choices.

237
00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.220
Also, I want to say here's just a little hint.

238
00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:20.300
The term animal, it's a very loose term.

239
00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:23.020
You could include humans.

240
00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:25.380
Well, that's on the liSt.

241
00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:26.060
Oh.

242
00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:30.140
Humans is the second most dangerous thing to humans.

243
00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:34.820
But there is something that is the most dangerous thing to humans.

244
00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:37.120
More dangerous than a human?

245
00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:39.800
And it's more of an insect.

246
00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:42.020
It kills more people each year.

247
00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:42.340
Mosquito?

248
00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:43.180
Yes.

249
00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:44.280
Yes.

250
00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:45.620
You gave me a lot of clues.

251
00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:46.240
I did.

252
00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:47.160
You gave me a lot of clues.

253
00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.080
Mosquitoes carry life-threatening diseases, and those diseases kill about three quarters

254
00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:53.660
of a million people every year.

255
00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:56.960
Then the next dangerous thing to humans is humans.

256
00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:58.720
Number three, wild boar?

257
00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:01.500
Number three is the freshwater snail.

258
00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:02.800
What?

259
00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:03.440
Yeah.

