And Aaron is still like, ah!

761
00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:02.240
She's like, maybe he's an old man with a beard.

762
00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:09.820
Ben, for my head to be that high above the partition, I was standing on an Apple box.

763
00:34:10.200 --> 00:34:11.100
You were.

764
00:34:11.320 --> 00:34:11.460
Yeah.

765
00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:13.880
So that we could see you scoff and disguSt.

766
00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:16.260
Because otherwise you just would have seen the top of my forehead.

767
00:34:16.700 --> 00:34:21.160
Did you notice that at eight minutes, Jordan is sitting at Dwight's desk?

768
00:34:21.700 --> 00:34:23.620
She has taken over his desk.

769
00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:24.780
Yes, I did notice.

770
00:34:25.220 --> 00:34:26.960
Well, now we're back in Dwight's office.

771
00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:31.420
Dwight has called Jim in because he wants to talk to him because Joe is coming and he

772
00:34:31.420 --> 00:34:33.800
doesn't want Jim, you know, to cause any probleMs.

773
00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:34.480
That's right.

774
00:34:34.660 --> 00:34:36.140
No pranking with Joe here.

775
00:34:36.860 --> 00:34:38.480
Jim really takes in the room.

776
00:34:38.620 --> 00:34:41.640
He's like, wow, you know, this is quite the decor.

777
00:34:42.260 --> 00:34:44.840
And Dwight starts to point things out to him.

778
00:34:45.540 --> 00:34:50.100
Yes, this is when we learn that the gun on his desk is a Beaumont AdaMs.

779
00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:54.200
Yeah, he thought maybe, you know, Joe might like it, might give them something to talk about.

780
00:34:54.440 --> 00:34:55.920
Yeah, I guess she collects guns.

781
00:34:56.120 --> 00:34:56.320
Yeah.

782
00:34:56.320 --> 00:34:56.840
Okay.

783
00:34:57.320 --> 00:35:04.460
Also, this is when he mentions his rescue piranha as well as his desk, which is, he says,

784
00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:07.260
a replica of Uday Hussein's desk.

785
00:35:07.400 --> 00:35:09.020
He read an article in Newsweek.

786
00:35:09.540 --> 00:35:12.160
Here's something I remember about that desk, Angela.

787
00:35:12.420 --> 00:35:15.160
I remember that it was made out of wood.

788
00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:19.140
It was very light and they painted it to look like marble.

789
00:35:19.340 --> 00:35:24.700
And I would just go in and stare at it because I was so impressed with this painting job.

790
00:35:24.880 --> 00:35:25.740
I thought it was amazing.

791
00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:31.280
You know what I thought about when I rewatched this episode was if that desk was truly marble

792
00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:34.900
and that big, they would not have been able to get it through the door and in the room.

793
00:35:35.120 --> 00:35:38.180
You would have to build a room around that desk.

794
00:35:38.620 --> 00:35:44.240
Also, in the commentary, Justin and Mindy shared that it was Mindy's idea to have this desk.

795
00:35:44.240 --> 00:35:45.880
Oh, I love it.

796
00:35:45.980 --> 00:35:48.680
Well, this was a whole thing, this desk, how they built it.

797
00:35:48.780 --> 00:35:52.180
I asked Randy because I told him my memory of loving it.

798
00:35:52.420 --> 00:35:59.120
And he said the writers had originally pitched that the desk was like a solid black onyx.

799
00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:05.740
But Michael Gallenberg, our production designer, said that that would just suck out all the light in the room.

800
00:36:05.840 --> 00:36:07.260
It wouldn't look good on camera.

801
00:36:07.460 --> 00:36:11.060
And he thought it would be more visually interesting if we made it out of marble.

802
00:36:11.060 --> 00:36:14.520
So Tim James built it out of lumber.

803
00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:23.820
And then we hired this guy, Ron Henson, who is an amazing painter, to paint it to look like marble.

804
00:36:24.380 --> 00:36:28.160
Well, you would never know that that was like a fakie desk.

805
00:36:28.340 --> 00:36:29.280
It looks so good.

806
00:36:29.900 --> 00:36:34.520
Justin said there was a reason why they over-decorated Dwight's office.

807
00:36:35.340 --> 00:36:39.600
And it was because they wanted to, quote, unquote, hide the gun.

808
00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:41.840
He said that audiences are really smart.

809
00:36:42.140 --> 00:36:46.880
That audiences know if you introduce a gun into a storyline, that gun is going to go off.

810
00:36:47.160 --> 00:36:49.340
I guess it's called Chekhov's gun.

811
00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:51.460
It's like a writer's theory.

812
00:36:51.600 --> 00:36:51.960
Oh, yeah.

813
00:36:51.960 --> 00:37:02.440
Okay, so he thought that if he did everything he could to make the gun feel just like a minor prop, among other ridiculous props, maybe, just maybe,

814
00:37:02.660 --> 00:37:06.060
the fact that Dwight fires the gun later would be a surprise.

815
00:37:06.320 --> 00:37:09.580
Well, I think he did a good job because he put it in a glass box.

816
00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:11.660
I didn't think it was going to get used.

817
00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:15.640
I thought it was just another one of Dwight's swords and daggers and armor.

818
00:37:15.980 --> 00:37:16.240
Yeah.

819
00:37:16.660 --> 00:37:20.840
Justin said on the commentary, Jenna, that they broke down this episode so many times.

820
00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:28.460
It was a bit of a puzzle trying to figure out how to make Dwight the manager and what that would look like, but then also where would it go?

821
00:37:28.920 --> 00:37:35.160
And it was the writer Steve Healy's idea that Dwight would become manager and screw it up all in one week.

822
00:37:35.320 --> 00:37:36.380
Oh, that's fun.

823
00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:43.160
So you had to have a big thing that would, like, sabotage him being manager because he was probably going to be pretty good at it.

824
00:37:43.280 --> 00:37:43.560
Right.

825
00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:46.680
Well, in the next scene, Dwight's going to open up a package.

826
00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:50.800
And inside is a gun holster.

827
00:37:51.100 --> 00:37:52.520
From his Uncle Honk.

828
00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:53.280
Yes.

829
00:37:53.740 --> 00:37:55.420
And he's so excited.

830
00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:56.360
He's so touched.

831
00:37:56.560 --> 00:37:56.740
Yeah.

832
00:37:57.180 --> 00:37:59.620
Well, Dwight immediately takes his gun holster.

833
00:37:59.860 --> 00:38:00.960
He puts his gun in it.

834
00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:03.020
And he starts parading around the office.

835
00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:03.560
Yeah.

836
00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:06.360
Everyone's like, Dwight, what are you doing?

837
00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:08.840
You can't just walk around with a gun in the office.

838
00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:12.140
And he's like, but my Uncle Honk made this for me.

839
00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:13.480
This is so special.

840
00:38:13.480 --> 00:38:17.740
Yeah, he's like, you can't, like, just walk around without a gun in it.

841
00:38:17.920 --> 00:38:19.640
You have to put the gun in it.

842
00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:21.240
And everyone's, like, pitching ideas.

843
00:38:21.240 --> 00:38:22.760
Well, why don't you put your phone in it?

844
00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:24.300
Why don't you put a banana in it?

845
00:38:24.580 --> 00:38:27.840
I would have loved to seen him walking around with a banana in that holster.

846
00:38:28.020 --> 00:38:31.260
I thought it was a good suggestion, actually, I have to say.

847
00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:33.780
Pam is going to threaten to call Joe.

848
00:38:35.440 --> 00:38:37.520
And that's when Dwight is like, fine.

849
00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:40.720
And he does a little twirly thing with the gun.

850
00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:45.180
He's going to put it back in the holster, but instead it fires right into the ground,

851
00:38:45.460 --> 00:38:46.860
right next to Andy's ear.

852
00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:49.300
And Andy grabs his ear.

853
00:38:49.660 --> 00:38:53.940
On the commentary, they were like, this was a really strong cutaway because that was the

854
00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:54.460
act break.

855
00:38:55.160 --> 00:38:56.900
That's a pretty strong act break.

856
00:38:57.480 --> 00:38:59.940
Well, we got a lot of questions about this moment.

857
00:39:00.260 --> 00:39:03.200
First of all, Dash P from Connecticut said,

858
00:39:03.200 --> 00:39:08.680
Did Rain fire a blank or was the flash and gunshot sound added in post?

859
00:39:09.100 --> 00:39:11.060
No, that was not added in poSt.

860
00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:12.360
Rain fired a blank.

861
00:39:12.540 --> 00:39:14.180
That was a real working gun.

862
00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:20.140
Randy explained that a blank is a cartridge that contains no projectile.

863
00:39:20.260 --> 00:39:22.100
It just has gunpowder in it.

864
00:39:22.700 --> 00:39:28.900
We used what was called a quarter round, which holds about a fourth of the amount of gunpowder,

865
00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:29.560
I guess.

866
00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:32.700
But still, firing a blank can be really dangerous.

867
00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:36.220
And even a quarter round makes a very loud noise.

868
00:39:36.220 --> 00:39:38.440
It produces that muzzle flash.

869
00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:44.360
So in order to do this, the crew held multiple rehearsals without any cast on set.

870
00:39:44.760 --> 00:39:46.440
We had a full safety meeting.

871
00:39:46.440 --> 00:39:47.480
I remember that.

872
00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:52.040
And they handed out those special shooter's earplugs for us all to wear.

873
00:39:52.220 --> 00:39:52.700
Just for the sound.

874
00:39:52.700 --> 00:39:54.100
Because it was a very loud noise.

875
00:39:54.240 --> 00:39:55.160
It's a very loud pop.

876
00:39:55.500 --> 00:39:57.500
And Randy said we did it in one take.

877
00:39:58.360 --> 00:40:02.940
Well, one of the things they talked about on the commentary was the idea that Dwight does

878
00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:08.420
something that's definitely going to get him removed from acting manager, but also making

879
00:40:08.420 --> 00:40:12.160
sure the way they shot it, it was very clear that no one was injured.

880
00:40:12.980 --> 00:40:17.620
We also got a fan question from Zach T in Illinois, who said,

881
00:40:18.040 --> 00:40:24.860
regarding Dwight's gun, after doing some research, it seems that the gun that Dwight used resembles a

882
00:40:24.860 --> 00:40:28.660
Remington 1875 American-made revolver.

883
00:40:29.000 --> 00:40:35.080
However, Dwight refers to it as a Beaumont Adams, which was a British service revolver from the

884
00:40:35.080 --> 00:40:36.480
mid-late 19th century.

885
00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:41.020
That actually looks very different than the gun that was used in this episode.

886
00:40:41.460 --> 00:40:47.020
What gun was used for the prop, and how did the writers decide on calling it a Beaumont

887
00:40:47.020 --> 00:40:51.140
Adams, despite the fact that it looks nothing like a Beaumont Adams?

888
00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:53.880
Well, Zach, good catch.

889
00:40:54.100 --> 00:40:55.840
That was not a Beaumont AdaMs.

890
00:40:56.140 --> 00:41:03.320
So we hired an armorer for this scene, and he informed us that there are no credible working

891
00:41:03.320 --> 00:41:05.260
replicas of a Beaumont AdaMs.

892
00:41:05.260 --> 00:41:06.760
It, like, couldn't be found.

893
00:41:06.940 --> 00:41:10.980
He said, we could have one built, but it would take months.

894
00:41:11.180 --> 00:41:11.800
Oh, wow.

895
00:41:12.020 --> 00:41:17.680
So the producers decided to go with this Remington 1875.

896
00:41:18.200 --> 00:41:24.360
It was a modern-day build of a classic revolver, and because this was not some sort of antique

897
00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:28.880
gun, but a modern-made gun, it was considered safer to handle.

898
00:41:29.080 --> 00:41:30.560
It could really fire a blank.

899
00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:32.760
So we went with that.

900
00:41:32.760 --> 00:41:36.980
But the writers chose to keep the Beaumont Adams reference just because they thought it

901
00:41:36.980 --> 00:41:41.300
was funny, and that it sounded like something that Joe might recognize and appreciate.

902
00:41:42.220 --> 00:41:47.000
Justin said, internet gun aficionados have called them out on this cheat before.

903
00:41:47.540 --> 00:41:50.300
You know, Angela, I remember it was loud.

904
00:41:50.560 --> 00:41:51.220
Do you remember that?

905
00:41:51.480 --> 00:41:51.660
Yeah.

906
00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:53.820
I mean, we didn't have to pretend to jump.

907
00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:56.120
No, all of our reactions were very real.

908
00:41:56.240 --> 00:41:56.940
For sure.

909
00:41:57.960 --> 00:42:04.360
At 11 minutes and 26 seconds, Oscar is going to examine the gunshot hole that is in the floor.

910
00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:05.120
Mm-hmm.

911
00:42:05.320 --> 00:42:05.640
Right?

912
00:42:05.920 --> 00:42:06.220
Yes.

913
00:42:06.220 --> 00:42:09.920
And he's going to say, look at that, hardwood floors underneath this carpet.

914
00:42:10.120 --> 00:42:10.600
Mm-hmm.

915
00:42:10.780 --> 00:42:11.460
All along.

916
00:42:11.720 --> 00:42:17.160
I would like to point out that while Oscar is examining the damage to the floors, you can

917
00:42:17.160 --> 00:42:19.740
see that John had a heater under his desk.

918
00:42:20.160 --> 00:42:20.640
What?

919
00:42:21.060 --> 00:42:21.460
Yeah.

920
00:42:21.920 --> 00:42:22.460
John?

921
00:42:22.720 --> 00:42:26.860
All this time, I thought it was just us, but John has a heater under his desk.

922
00:42:27.020 --> 00:42:27.740
I took a picture.

923
00:42:28.320 --> 00:42:31.360
I am like, I'm shocked right now.

924
00:42:31.360 --> 00:42:32.960
I was shocked as well.

925
00:42:33.180 --> 00:42:37.240
I know I should be, like, focusing on the episode and this big plot point just happened,

926
00:42:37.240 --> 00:42:40.600
but I couldn't get my eyes off a heater under John's desk.

927
00:42:40.680 --> 00:42:41.080
Wow.

928
00:42:41.660 --> 00:42:42.480
Check it out.

929
00:42:43.620 --> 00:42:44.600
And it's on.

930
00:42:44.860 --> 00:42:45.040
Yeah.

931
00:42:45.200 --> 00:42:47.000
The little red light is on.

932
00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:50.860
Well, Toby's going to come in to see what's going on.

933
00:42:51.280 --> 00:42:54.040
Kelly's going to say that Dwight went on a shooting spree.

934
00:42:54.600 --> 00:42:57.200
Background catch at 11 minutes, 45 seconds.

935
00:42:57.660 --> 00:43:00.760
Did you guys catch that Dwight hands his gun to Creed?

936
00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:02.860
And I guess Creed is going to get rid of it?

937
00:43:03.520 --> 00:43:03.960
Yeah.

938
00:43:04.220 --> 00:43:07.940
I mean, if anyone knows how to get rid of a gun in this office, it's probably Creed.

939
00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:09.160
Just such a great...

940
00:43:09.160 --> 00:43:11.680
Don't you think maybe this isn't the first gun he's gotten rid of?

941
00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:12.600
I'm just saying.

942
00:43:12.860 --> 00:43:14.160
I mean, he lives under the desk.

943
00:43:14.160 --> 00:43:15.640
He has a whole other life in Canada.

944
00:43:15.940 --> 00:43:16.600
We don't know.

945
00:43:16.720 --> 00:43:17.240
We don't know.

946
00:43:17.940 --> 00:43:23.400
Well, Oscar wants to look up some of Andy's symptoms on WebMD, which gets some great

947
00:43:23.400 --> 00:43:24.500
snark from Angela.

948
00:43:24.500 --> 00:43:28.460
This is one of my favorite Angela Martin lines of all of season seven.

949
00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:33.440
In the script, it was written like this, and we shot it exactly as it was written.

950
00:43:34.060 --> 00:43:35.800
Oscar says, I'll look on WebMD.

951
00:43:35.920 --> 00:43:36.700
What are your symptoms?

952
00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:40.020
Angela, in parentheses, sarcastic.

953
00:43:40.780 --> 00:43:44.200
Oh, everybody, Oscar found a reason to look on WebMD.

954
00:43:44.560 --> 00:43:47.040
I love the assumed history of these two characters.

955
00:43:47.280 --> 00:43:52.640
I love knowing that he looks on WebMD so much that it has driven her crazy for years.

956
00:43:52.640 --> 00:43:59.300
I think it's lines like this that really made this feel like a nostalgic old office episode.

957
00:43:59.560 --> 00:44:01.740
And also like the stuff at the copier.

958
00:44:01.880 --> 00:44:06.920
We just brought back, I feel like, some of that mundane office work kind of stuff.

959
00:44:07.040 --> 00:44:07.220
Yeah.

960
00:44:07.460 --> 00:44:08.820
And relationship stuff.

961
00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:09.580
Yeah.

962
00:44:09.580 --> 00:44:19.500
I want you to know that when Toby gets excited to go get his new safety binder, I really felt that I would be excited to use a new binder.

963
00:44:19.780 --> 00:44:24.000
You know, I have a lot of stuff in my house that is for various emergencies.

964
00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:31.040
And I'm not saying that I want a zombie apocalypse or that I want any kind of apocalypse or end of times.

965
00:44:31.320 --> 00:44:37.940
But if it happened, I would be so excited to grab my expired medications book bag.

966
00:44:38.660 --> 00:44:41.640
You know, I would just be like, I knew it!

967
00:44:41.800 --> 00:44:45.440
I really can't wait for you to meet the character of Bill in The Last of Us.

968
00:44:45.540 --> 00:44:47.040
I need you to meet Bill.

969
00:44:47.220 --> 00:44:47.480
Okay.

970
00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:49.000
I'm not going to say anything else.

971
00:44:49.200 --> 00:44:49.380
All right.

972
00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:54.840
But speaking of Toby and how excited he was, you know, to have his emergency binder.

973
00:44:56.120 --> 00:44:59.760
Jenna, do you remember when we were filming this when Paul came out to say all that?

974
00:44:59.880 --> 00:45:01.060
He had to address the bullpen.

975
00:45:01.220 --> 00:45:01.440
Yes.

976
00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:04.200
And he mumbled so badly we couldn't understand him.

977
00:45:04.620 --> 00:45:06.020
We even improvised.

978
00:45:06.180 --> 00:45:07.660
What are you saying, Toby?

979
00:45:08.040 --> 00:45:08.920
Speak up!

980
00:45:09.580 --> 00:45:11.240
We started laughing so hard.

981
00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:16.960
And I don't know if it was Paul or Paul being Toby, but he really struggled getting that line out.

982
00:45:16.960 --> 00:45:18.980
I feel like that made it onto the bloopers.

983
00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:26.300
It definitely made it on, you know, they would do blooper reels and stuff for our end of season cast parties that we would have.

984
00:45:26.440 --> 00:45:28.420
And I remember it was definitely on there.

985
00:45:29.500 --> 00:45:37.540
Dwight is now going to do his best Michael, which is where he calls a conference room meeting and creates a character to cover up his screw up.

986
00:45:37.660 --> 00:45:39.160
Gun safety Dwight!

987
00:45:39.520 --> 00:45:39.860
Yeah.

988
00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:41.660
I'm the rootinist toot.

989
00:45:41.720 --> 00:45:42.740
And he just can't do it.

990
00:45:42.840 --> 00:45:43.760
He fails immediately.

991
00:45:44.080 --> 00:45:44.460
He does.

992
00:45:44.660 --> 00:45:46.580
He does give a pretty great speech, though.

993
00:45:46.580 --> 00:45:47.180
He says,

994
00:45:47.500 --> 00:45:50.820
Managing you for this last week has been the greatest honor of my life.

995
00:45:50.960 --> 00:45:53.980
And if you ruin this, I will burn this office to the ground.

996
00:45:54.140 --> 00:45:59.200
And I mean that figuratively, not literally, because you guys are so, so important to me.

997
00:45:59.480 --> 00:46:00.620
I love you guys.

998
00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:01.860
But don't cross me.

999
00:46:02.140 --> 00:46:03.120
But you're the beSt.

1000
00:46:03.620 --> 00:46:07.420
Rain said that that final monologue at the end was really hard for him to get through.

