I have a deep dive on burst eardruMs.

1013
00:46:40.200 --> 00:46:45.720
Wait, before you go on this deep dive, I have to tell you one thing.

1014
00:46:46.380 --> 00:46:52.220
Justin said on the commentary, he said they were so worried that people were going to get grossed out by this injury.

1015
00:46:52.380 --> 00:46:52.760
Uh-huh.

1016
00:46:53.220 --> 00:46:55.840
That they were trying to find different ways to word it.

1017
00:46:56.000 --> 00:46:59.980
And at one point in the script, they had Andy saying, my ear don't work so good.

1018
00:47:00.660 --> 00:47:01.100
Oh.

1019
00:47:01.100 --> 00:47:02.500
My ear no-do work.

1020
00:47:02.620 --> 00:47:04.300
They had all these different phrases.

1021
00:47:05.160 --> 00:47:09.400
But ultimately, they had to say, you know, that he's going to have this injured eardrum.

1022
00:47:09.760 --> 00:47:13.980
And I just feel like I have to bring that up because I don't know what to expect from this deep dive.

1023
00:47:14.140 --> 00:47:19.120
If they were worried, a group of writers who had researched it, that people were going to get grossed out.

1024
00:47:19.940 --> 00:47:24.080
I'm not going to explain everything that happens when you have a burst eardrum.

1025
00:47:24.200 --> 00:47:24.620
Thank you.

1026
00:47:24.920 --> 00:47:30.880
Except to say that the symptoms that Andy complains about are symptoms of a burst eardrum.

1027
00:47:31.100 --> 00:47:35.560
The ringing in the ear, the hearing loss, the pain, all of those things.

1028
00:47:36.140 --> 00:47:41.920
What I got curious about was, can a loud noise burst your eardrum?

1029
00:47:42.040 --> 00:47:43.040
Is that true?

1030
00:47:43.360 --> 00:47:47.300
Is that possible that this gunshot sound burst his eardrum?

1031
00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:48.020
Sure.

1032
00:47:48.020 --> 00:47:49.260
I'm going to say sure.

1033
00:47:49.640 --> 00:47:50.800
The answer is yes.

1034
00:47:50.800 --> 00:48:06.000
You can get a perforated eardrum, which is a burst eardrum, from a loud sound, a foreign object in your ear, like a Q-tip, head trauma, an ear infection, or rapid pressure changes.

1035
00:48:06.840 --> 00:48:08.480
Like on a plane.

1036
00:48:08.480 --> 00:48:12.620
Or going down into the ocean, you know, if you dive down too faSt.

1037
00:48:12.840 --> 00:48:13.060
Yeah.

1038
00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:14.640
Or a submarine, sure.

1039
00:48:15.260 --> 00:48:18.420
Just going to yell different modes of transportation.

1040
00:48:18.760 --> 00:48:18.880
Sure.

1041
00:48:19.080 --> 00:48:20.700
To either up high or down low.

1042
00:48:21.340 --> 00:48:29.020
But getting back to this idea of your eardrum rupturing from a loud noise, I wanted to know, like, how loud does the noise have to be?

1043
00:48:29.020 --> 00:48:37.200
And, according to Better Hearing of Austin, it would have to be 165 decibels or more.

1044
00:48:37.660 --> 00:48:46.120
And that would be consistent with a gunshot at close range, fireworks, or extremely loud music.

1045
00:48:46.360 --> 00:48:49.160
